    "knowledge_point": "Using The Law of Total Probability Stated in Terms of Conditional Probability",
    "problem": "A certain disease is present in $\\frac{1}{10}$ of the population. A test to detect the disease gives a positive result in $\\frac{9}{10}$ of the people with the disease and in $\\frac{1}{10}$ of the people without the disease. What is the probability that a randomly selected patient tests positive for the disease?",
    "worked_solution": "Let $A$ be the event that the test is positive for the selected patient, and let $D$ be the event that the patient has the disease. Then, ${D}^{′}$ is the event that the patient does not have the disease.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected patient gives a positive result, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(A)=P(A|D)P(D)+P(A|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since the disease is present in $\\frac{1}{10}$ of the population, we have\n\n$$\nP(D)=\\frac{1}{10},\\,P({D}^{′})=\\frac{9}{10}.\n$$\n\n- Since the test gives a positive result in $\\frac{9}{10}$ of the people with the disease, and in $\\frac{1}{10}$ of the people without the disease, we have\n\n$$\nP(A|D)=\\frac{9}{10},\\,P(A|{D}^{′})=\\frac{1}{10}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected patient tests positive for the disease is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A) & =P(A|D)P(D)+P(A|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{9}{10})(\\frac{1}{10})+(\\frac{1}{10})(\\frac{9}{10}) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{100}+\\frac{9}{100} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{100} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{50}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
